{"id": "SATM2091JX6", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 11)$ and $(5, 29)$. What is the equation of this line in slope-intercept form?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = 6x - 1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 6x + 1$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = \\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{32}{3}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = -6x + 23$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{29 - 11}{5 - 2} = \\frac{18}{3} = 6$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 11)$: $y - 11 = 6(x - 2)$, which simplifies to $y = 6x - 12 + 11 = 6x - 1$. The equation $y = 6x + 1$ results from a sign error when computing $-12 + 11$, incorrectly obtaining $+1$ instead of $-1$. The equation $y = \\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{32}{3}$ reflects a concept gap, using the reciprocal of the slope instead of the slope itself. The equation $y = -6x + 23$ arises from an equation setup error, negating the slope when applying point-slope form."}
{"id": "SATM20FFV4W", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 13)$ and $(5, 31)$. What is the $x$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}, 0\\right)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{1}{6}, 0\\right)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\left(-\\frac{7}{6}, 0\\right)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{7}{6}, 0\\right)$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{31-13}{5-2} = \\frac{18}{3} = 6$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 13)$: $y - 13 = 6(x - 2)$, which gives $y = 6x + 1$. Setting $y = 0$: $0 = 6x + 1$, so $x = -\\frac{1}{6}$. The $x$-intercept is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}, 0\\right)$. The value $\\left(\\frac{1}{6}, 0\\right)$ comes from a sign error when solving $6x = -1$. The value $\\left(-\\frac{7}{6}, 0\\right)$ results from computing the $y$-intercept as $b = 13 - 6 = 7$ instead of $b = 13 - 6(2) = 1$, then solving $0 = 6x + 7$ to get $x = -\\frac{7}{6}$. The value $\\left(\\frac{7}{6}, 0\\right)$ comes from using $b = -7$ instead of $+1$ and then also making a sign error when solving."}
{"id": "SATM20JQNTO", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A line in the $xy$-plane passes through the points $(1, 9)$ and $(4, 21)$. What is the slope of the line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "4", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{21 - 9}{4 - 1} = \\frac{12}{3} = 4$."}
{"id": "SATM20P46C0", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $x$-intercept of the line $7x + 3y = 63$ ?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$9$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$21$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$63$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The $x$-intercept occurs where $y = 0$. Substituting $y = 0$ into $7x + 3(0) = 63$ gives $7x = 63$, so $x = 9$. The value $21$ results from setting $x = 0$ instead, which gives the $y$-intercept ($3y = 63$, $y = 21$) rather than the $x$-intercept. The value $63$ results from setting $y = 0$ to get $7x = 63$ but reporting $63$ instead of dividing by $7$. The value $3$ results from first correctly finding $x = 9$ and then erroneously dividing by $3$ (the coefficient of $y$), or equivalently computing $63 \\div 21 = 3$."}
{"id": "SATM20RDIFH", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Which of the following equations represents the line that passes through the points $(3, 11)$ and $(7, 31)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = 4x - 1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 5x - 15$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = 5x - 4$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = 5x + 4$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{31 - 11}{7 - 3} = \\frac{20}{4} = 5$. Using point-slope form with $(3, 11)$: $y - 11 = 5(x - 3)$, which gives $y = 5x - 15 + 11 = 5x - 4$. The equation $y = 4x - 1$ results from miscalculating the denominator $7 - 3$ as $5$ instead of $4$, giving a slope of $\\frac{20}{5} = 4$. The equation $y = 5x - 15$ uses the correct slope but omits adding back the $y$-coordinate $11$ after applying point-slope form. The equation $y = 5x + 4$ has a sign error in the $y$-intercept computation."}
{"id": "SATM20RQ8VK", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $m$ is defined by the equation $5x + 2y = 18$. Line $n$ is perpendicular to line $m$ and passes through the point $(10, -3)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $n$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$1$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$22$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting line $m$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = -\\frac{5}{2}x + 9$, so the slope of line $m$ is $-\\frac{5}{2}$. A line perpendicular to $m$ has slope $\\frac{2}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(10, -3)$: $y - (-3) = \\frac{2}{5}(x - 10)$, which gives $y + 3 = \\frac{2}{5}x - 4$, so $y = \\frac{2}{5}x - 7$. The $y$-intercept is $-7$. The value $1$ comes from using the slope $-\\frac{2}{5}$ instead of $\\frac{2}{5}$, taking the reciprocal but not negating; this gives $y + 3 = -\\frac{2}{5}(x - 10) = -\\frac{2}{5}x + 4$, so $y = -\\frac{2}{5}x + 1$ with $y$-intercept $1$. The value $7$ results from a sign error, negating $-7$ to get $7$. The value $22$ arises from using the same slope $-\\frac{5}{2}$ as line $m$ (a parallel line rather than perpendicular), giving $y + 3 = -\\frac{5}{2}(x - 10) = -\\frac{5}{2}x + 25$, so $y = -\\frac{5}{2}x + 22$ with $y$-intercept $22$."}
{"id": "SATM20SQ00T", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line in the $xy$-plane passes through the points $(2, 17)$ and $(6, 41)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "5", "explanation": "The slope is $(41 - 17) \\div (6 - 2) = 24 \\div 4 = 6$. Using point $(2, 17)$: $17 = 6(2) + b$, so $b = 17 - 12 = 5$. The $y$-intercept is 5."}
{"id": "SATM215AO9B", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "In the equation $4x + 9y = 83$, if $y = 7$, what is the value of $x$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "5", "explanation": "Substituting $y = 7$ gives $4x + 9(7) = 83$, which simplifies to $4x + 63 = 83$. Subtracting $63$ from both sides yields $4x = 20$, and dividing by $4$ gives $x = 5$. A common error is a sign mistake when subtracting, such as computing $83 + 63 = 146$ and dividing by $4$ to get $36.5$. Another error is dividing $83$ by $4$ directly without subtracting $63$ first."}
{"id": "SATM21BAVB3", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The graph of the equation \\(ax - 5y = 30\\) has an \\(x\\)-intercept at \\((6, 0)\\). The graph also passes through the point \\((1, b)\\). What is the value of \\(b\\)?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "\\(-6\\)"}, {"key": "B", "text": "\\(-5\\)"}, {"key": "C", "text": "\\(1\\)"}, {"key": "D", "text": "\\(5\\)"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Use the \\(x\\)-intercept \\((6, 0)\\) to find \\(a\\): \\(6a - 5(0) = 30\\), so \\(a = 5\\). The equation becomes \\(5x - 5y = 30\\), or equivalently \\(x - y = 6\\). Substituting \\(x = 1\\): \\(1 - b = 6\\), which gives \\(b = -5\\). The value \\(-6\\) results from confusing the \\(x\\)-intercept value with \\(b\\). The value 1 arises from stopping after finding \\(a = 5\\) and using \\(x = 1\\) as the answer. The value 5 comes from a sign error when solving \\(1 - b = 6\\)."}
{"id": "SATM21FBGMG", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "What is the $x$-intercept of the line $4x - 7y = 28$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(4, 0)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(7, 0)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(0, -4)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(-7, 0)$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Setting $y = 0$ in $4x - 7(0) = 28$ gives $4x = 28$, so $x = 7$. The $x$-intercept is $(7, 0)$. The point $(4, 0)$ confuses the coefficient of $x$ with the intercept value. The point $(0, -4)$ is the $y$-intercept (found by setting $x = 0$), not the $x$-intercept. The point $(-7, 0)$ introduces a sign error when dividing."}
{"id": "SATM21FGHB4", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $y$-intercept of the line $3x - 5y = 45$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(0, -15)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(0, -9)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(0, 9)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(15, 0)$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The $y$-intercept occurs where $x = 0$. Substituting into $3(0) - 5y = 45$ gives $-5y = 45$, so $y = -9$, and the intercept is $(0, -9)$. The point $(0, -15)$ results from dividing 45 by the coefficient of $x$ instead of the coefficient of $y$, a concept gap. The point $(0, 9)$ comes from a sign error, dropping the negative when dividing $45$ by $-5$. The point $(15, 0)$ is the $x$-intercept, indicating the question was misread as asking for the $x$-intercept instead of the $y$-intercept."}
{"id": "SATM21OEBBB", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "Which of the following ordered pairs $(x, y)$ satisfies the equation $3x + 7y = 41$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(2, 5)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(3, 7)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(5, 2)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(7, 3)$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Substituting $(2, 5)$: $3(2) + 7(5) = 6 + 35 = 41$, which equals the right side. Substituting $(3, 7)$: $3(3) + 7(7) = 9 + 49 = 58 \\neq 41$, so this pair reflects an equation setup error. Substituting $(5, 2)$: $3(5) + 7(2) = 15 + 14 = 29 \\neq 41$; this pair swaps the correct coordinates, a misread of which value corresponds to $x$ and $y$. Substituting $(7, 3)$: $3(7) + 7(3) = 21 + 21 = 42 \\neq 41$, an arithmetic slip that is off by one."}
{"id": "SATM21R6TME", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(0, 7)$ and $(3, 16)$. What is the slope of this line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "3", "explanation": "The slope of a line through two points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ is $\\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$. Substituting the given points gives $\\frac{16 - 7}{3 - 0} = \\frac{9}{3} = 3$."}
{"id": "SATM21SWADW", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A line is perpendicular to $5x - 3y = 17$ and passes through the point $(7, -2)$. What is the $x$-intercept of this line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "11/3", "explanation": "Rewriting $5x - 3y = 17$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = \\frac{5}{3}x - \\frac{17}{3}$, so the slope of this line is $\\frac{5}{3}$. A line perpendicular to it has slope $-\\frac{3}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(7, -2)$: $y + 2 = -\\frac{3}{5}(x - 7)$. Distributing gives $y + 2 = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{21}{5}$, so $y = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{11}{5}$. Setting $y = 0$ to find the $x$-intercept: $0 = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{11}{5}$, which gives $\\frac{3}{5}x = \\frac{11}{5}$, so $x = \\frac{11}{3}$. A common sign error is using the slope $\\frac{3}{5}$ (the ordinary reciprocal without negating) for the perpendicular line, which would yield a different intercept. Another error is confusing the perpendicular slope with $-\\frac{5}{3}$ (negating without reciprocating), which also produces a wrong answer."}
