{"id": "SATM221PUJU", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 13)$ and $(6, 29)$. Which of the following is the equation of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = -4x + 21$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = \\frac{1}{4}x + \\frac{25}{2}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = 4x - 5$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = 4x + 5$"}], "correct_answer": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is $y = 4x + 5$. The slope is $\\frac{29 - 13}{6 - 2} = \\frac{16}{4} = 4$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 13)$: $y - 13 = 4(x - 2)$, which simplifies to $y = 4x - 8 + 13 = 4x + 5$. The equation $y = -4x + 21$ uses a negated slope; although it passes through $(2, 13)$, it does not pass through $(6, 29)$. The equation $y = \\frac{1}{4}x + \\frac{25}{2}$ uses the reciprocal of the slope instead of the slope itself. The equation $y = 4x - 5$ has the correct slope but an incorrect $y$-intercept from a sign error when simplifying $-8 + 13$."}
{"id": "SATM22B9WSR", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Line $m$ passes through the points $(-3, 11)$ and $(5, -13)$. What is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of line $m$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "2/3", "explanation": "The slope of line $m$ is $\\frac{-13 - 11}{5 - (-3)} = \\frac{-24}{8} = -3$. Using point-slope form with the point $(-3, 11)$: $y - 11 = -3(x - (-3))$, which simplifies to $y - 11 = -3(x + 3) = -3x - 9$, so $y = -3x + 2$. Setting $y = 0$ to find the $x$-intercept gives $0 = -3x + 2$, so $3x = 2$ and $x = \\frac{2}{3}$. The $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept is $\\frac{2}{3}$."}
