{"id": "SATM22ELV2T", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $y$-intercept of the line $13x + 7y = 91$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$13$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$91$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{91}{13}$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The $y$-intercept occurs when $x = 0$. Substituting: $13(0) + 7y = 91$, so $7y = 91$ and $y = 13$. The value 7 confuses the coefficient of $y$ with the intercept value. The value 91 ignores the coefficient of $y$ and treats the constant as the intercept directly. The value $\\frac{91}{13}$ finds the $x$-intercept (setting $y = 0$) instead of the $y$-intercept."}
{"id": "SATM22G7WWP", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 13)$ and $(5, 31)$. What is the $x$-intercept of this line? Express your answer as a fraction if necessary.", "options": [], "correct_answer": "-1/6", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{31 - 13}{5 - 2} = \\frac{18}{3} = 6$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 13)$: $y - 13 = 6(x - 2)$, which simplifies to $y = 6x + 1$. Setting $y = 0$: $0 = 6x + 1$, so $x = -\\frac{1}{6}$."}
{"id": "SATM22JGB26", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $9x + 7y = 63$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(4, -3)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\left(0, -\\frac{55}{9}\\right)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\left(0, \\frac{55}{9}\\right)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\left(0, -\\frac{1}{9}\\right)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\left(0, \\frac{1}{9}\\right)$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $7y = -9x + 63$, so $y = -\\frac{9}{7}x + 9$. The slope of $p$ is $-\\frac{9}{7}$, so the slope of a perpendicular line is $\\frac{7}{9}$. Using the point $(4, -3)$: $-3 = \\frac{7}{9}(4) + b$, giving $-3 = \\frac{28}{9} + b$, so $b = -3 - \\frac{28}{9} = -\\frac{27}{9} - \\frac{28}{9} = -\\frac{55}{9}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\left(0, -\\frac{55}{9}\\right)$. The value $\\left(0, \\frac{55}{9}\\right)$ results from dropping the negative sign on $-3$ and computing $3 + \\frac{28}{9} = \\frac{55}{9}$. The value $\\left(0, -\\frac{1}{9}\\right)$ comes from incorrectly computing $-\\frac{27}{9} + \\frac{28}{9} = \\frac{1}{9}$ and then negating. The value $\\left(0, \\frac{1}{9}\\right)$ results from the same arithmetic error without the final negation."}
