{"id": "SATM23LQE9M", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $5x - 2y = 9$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(4, -3)$. Which of the following is the equation of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$2x + 5y = -7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$2x + 5y = 23$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$5x - 2y = 26$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$2x - 5y = 23$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $y = \\frac{5}{2}x - \\frac{9}{2}$, so its slope is $\\frac{5}{2}$. A perpendicular line has slope $-\\frac{2}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(4, -3)$: $y + 3 = -\\frac{2}{5}(x - 4)$, giving $y = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{8}{5} - 3 = -\\frac{2}{5}x - \\frac{7}{5}$. Multiplying by $5$: $5y = -2x - 7$, so $2x + 5y = -7$. The equation $2x + 5y = 23$ results from a sign error when substituting the point, using $y - 3$ instead of $y + 3$. The equation $5x - 2y = 26$ uses the original slope $\\frac{5}{2}$ rather than the perpendicular slope, showing a conceptual gap about perpendicular slopes. The equation $2x - 5y = 23$ uses slope $\\frac{2}{5}$ (positive) instead of $-\\frac{2}{5}$, omitting the negative in the negative reciprocal."}
{"id": "SATM23NSU12", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "What is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the line that passes through the points $(2, 9)$ and $(6, -3)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$1$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$5$"}], "correct_answer": "D", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{-3 - 9}{6 - 2} = \\frac{-12}{4} = -3$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 9)$: $y - 9 = -3(x - 2)$, which simplifies to $y = -3x + 15$. Setting $y = 0$: $0 = -3x + 15$, so $x = 5$. The value $-1$ results from reversing the order of subtraction in the denominator of the slope (computing $\\frac{12}{-4} \\cdot (-1)$ erroneously as slope $3$), getting $y = 3x + 3$, and then solving $0 = 3x + 3$ to obtain $x = -1$. The value $1$ results from a sign error when computing $b$: using $9 = -3(2) + b$ but writing $9 = 6 + b$ instead of $9 = -6 + b$, giving $b = 3$ and the equation $y = -3x + 3$, so $x = 1$. The value $3$ results from confusing the $y$-intercept value $b = 3$ (from the sign error) with the $x$-intercept itself."}
{"id": "SATM23OJQW3", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A line passes through the point $(2, -5)$ and is perpendicular to the line $4x - 7y = 28$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-\\dfrac{3}{2}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\dfrac{3}{2}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\dfrac{17}{2}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\dfrac{7}{4}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting $4x - 7y = 28$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = \\frac{4}{7}x - 4$, so the slope is $\\frac{4}{7}$. The perpendicular slope is $-\\frac{7}{4}$. Using point-slope form with $(2, -5)$: $y + 5 = -\\frac{7}{4}(x - 2)$, which gives $y = -\\frac{7}{4}x + \\frac{7}{2} - 5 = -\\frac{7}{4}x - \\frac{3}{2}$. The $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{3}{2}$. The value $\\frac{3}{2}$ results from a sign error on the constant. The value $-\\frac{17}{2}$ comes from an arithmetic slip adding $\\frac{7}{2}$ and $-5$ incorrectly. The value $\\frac{7}{4}$ confuses the perpendicular slope with the $y$-intercept."}
{"id": "SATM23PRIFJ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $9x - 4y = 36$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(0, 7)$. Which of the following is the equation of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = -\\frac{4}{9}x + 7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = \\frac{9}{4}x + 7$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = \\frac{4}{9}x + 7$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = -\\frac{9}{4}x + 7$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting $9x - 4y = 36$ in slope-intercept form: $-4y = -9x + 36$, so $y = \\frac{9}{4}x - 9$. The slope of line $p$ is $\\frac{9}{4}$. A perpendicular line has slope equal to the negative reciprocal: $-\\frac{4}{9}$. Using the point $(0, 7)$, the equation is $y = -\\frac{4}{9}x + 7$. The equation $y = \\frac{9}{4}x + 7$ uses the original slope, which gives a parallel line. The equation $y = \\frac{4}{9}x + 7$ takes the reciprocal but omits the sign change. The equation $y = -\\frac{9}{4}x + 7$ negates the original slope without taking the reciprocal."}
{"id": "SATM23ZJ6ET", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(-1, 4)$ and $(3, -8)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(0, -1)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(0, 1)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(0, 7)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(0, -2)$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{-8 - 4}{3 - (-1)} = \\frac{-12}{4} = -3$. Using point-slope form with $(-1, 4)$: $y - 4 = -3(x + 1)$, which gives $y = -3x - 3 + 4 = -3x + 1$. The $y$-intercept is $(0, 1)$. The intercept $(0, -1)$ results from a sign error when simplifying $-3 + 4$, obtaining $-1$ instead of $1$. The intercept $(0, 7)$ comes from computing $y - 4 = -3(x - 1)$ instead of $y - 4 = -3(x + 1)$, mishandling the sign of the $x$-coordinate, which gives $y = -3x + 3 + 4 = -3x + 7$. The intercept $(0, -2)$ comes from averaging the two $y$-coordinates $\\frac{4 + (-8)}{2} = -2$ instead of determining the equation of the line, confusing the midpoint with the intercept."}
{"id": "SATM246Z3WH", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $l$ is perpendicular to the line $4x - 3y = 18$ and passes through the point $(6, -1)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $l$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-6$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{7}{2}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\frac{11}{2}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-9$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Rewriting $4x - 3y = 18$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = \\frac{4}{3}x - 6$, so the slope is $\\frac{4}{3}$. A line perpendicular to this has slope $-\\frac{3}{4}$. Using point-slope form with $(6, -1)$: $y + 1 = -\\frac{3}{4}(x - 6)$, which gives $y = -\\frac{3}{4}x + \\frac{18}{4} - 1 = -\\frac{3}{4}x + \\frac{7}{2}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{7}{2}$. The value $-6$ comes from using the $y$-intercept of the original line instead of computing the new one. The value $-\\frac{11}{2}$ results from using a positive slope $\\frac{3}{4}$ instead of $-\\frac{3}{4}$, producing $y = \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{11}{2}$. The value $-9$ results from using the original slope $\\frac{4}{3}$ rather than its negative reciprocal."}
{"id": "SATM24E01JS", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, -11)$ and $(6, 5)$. What is the $x$-intercept of this line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "19/4", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{5 - (-11)}{6 - 2} = \\frac{16}{4} = 4$. Using point-slope form with $(6, 5)$: $y - 5 = 4(x - 6)$, which simplifies to $y = 4x - 19$. Setting $y = 0$ to find the $x$-intercept: $0 = 4x - 19$, so $x = \\frac{19}{4}$. A common error is reversing the subtraction order in the slope formula, yielding $-4$ instead of $4$, which produces a different intercept. Another mistake is finding the $y$-intercept ($-19$) instead of the $x$-intercept."}
{"id": "SATM24Q2AKL", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ is perpendicular to the line $4x - 3y = 15$ and passes through the point $(8, -2)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $p$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(0, -8)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(0, -5)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(0, -4)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(0, 4)$"}], "correct_answer": "D", "explanation": "Rearranging $4x - 3y = 15$ gives $y = \\frac{4}{3}x - 5$, so the slope is $\\frac{4}{3}$. A perpendicular line has slope $-\\frac{3}{4}$. Using point-slope form with $(8, -2)$: $y + 2 = -\\frac{3}{4}(x - 8)$, which gives $y + 2 = -\\frac{3}{4}x + 6$, so $y = -\\frac{3}{4}x + 4$. The $y$-intercept is $(0, 4)$. The intercept $(0, -8)$ results from an equation setup error \u2014 using the reciprocal slope $\\frac{3}{4}$ instead of the negative reciprocal $-\\frac{3}{4}$, giving $y + 2 = \\frac{3}{4}(x - 8)$, then $y = \\frac{3}{4}x - 8$. The intercept $(0, -5)$ reflects a concept gap, simply reusing the $y$-intercept of the original line without computing the new equation. The intercept $(0, -4)$ comes from a sign error \u2014 arriving at $y = -\\frac{3}{4}x - 4$ by incorrectly subtracting $2$ from $-2$ when simplifying the point-slope equation."}
{"id": "SATM24TIXLQ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(1, 4)$ and $(3, 10)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-2$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$1$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$7$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is $1$. First, find the slope: $m = \\frac{10 - 4}{3 - 1} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3$. Using point-slope form with $(1, 4)$: $y - 4 = 3(x - 1)$, which gives $y = 3x + 1$. The $y$-intercept is $1$. $-2$ results from a sign error when solving for the intercept. $3$ is the slope, not the $y$-intercept, reflecting incomplete reasoning. $7$ comes from an arithmetic error such as adding the slope to the $y$-coordinate of the first point."}
{"id": "SATM24YLLJ3", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "Which of the following is the slope of the line represented by the equation $3x + 6y = 18$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-\\frac{1}{2}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$6$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting $3x + 6y = 18$ in slope-intercept form: subtract $3x$ from both sides to get $6y = -3x + 18$, then divide every term by $6$ to get $y = -\\frac{1}{2}x + 3$. The slope is $-\\frac{1}{2}$. The value $\\frac{1}{2}$ results from dropping the negative sign when dividing $-3$ by $6$. The value $3$ comes from reading the coefficient of $x$ in the original standard-form equation as the slope without converting to slope-intercept form. The value $6$ comes from reading the coefficient of $y$ in the original equation as the slope."}
{"id": "SATM25CJZCQ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $5x + 8y = 52$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(10, 3)$. What is the $x$-coordinate of the point where line $q$ crosses the $x$-axis?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "65/8", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $y = -\\frac{5}{8}x + \\frac{52}{8}$, so the slope of line $p$ is $-\\frac{5}{8}$. The slope of perpendicular line $q$ is the negative reciprocal, $\\frac{8}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(10, 3)$: $y - 3 = \\frac{8}{5}(x - 10)$, which simplifies to $y = \\frac{8}{5}x - 16 + 3 = \\frac{8}{5}x - 13$. Setting $y = 0$: $\\frac{8}{5}x = 13$, so $x = \\frac{13 \\times 5}{8} = \\frac{65}{8}$."}
{"id": "SATM25CQJX2", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The equation $ax + by = 24$ represents a line that passes through the point $(3, 6)$ and has a slope of $-2$. What is the value of $a + b$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$4$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$6$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$8$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$12$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is $6$. For the equation $ax + by = 24$, the slope is $-\\frac{a}{b}$. Setting this equal to $-2$ gives $\\frac{a}{b} = 2$, so $a = 2b$. Substituting the point $(3, 6)$ into the equation: $3a + 6b = 24$. Replacing $a$ with $2b$: $3(2b) + 6b = 24$, so $6b + 6b = 24$, giving $12b = 24$ and $b = 2$. Then $a = 2(2) = 4$, and $a + b = 4 + 2 = 6$. $4$ comes from finding only the value of $a$ without adding $b$. $8$ results from incorrectly setting up the slope relationship as $a = b$ instead of $a = 2b$. $12$ comes from an arithmetic error in solving the system."}
{"id": "SATM25FTJES", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 11)$ and $(5, 23)$. Which of the following equations represents this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = 4x - 3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 4x + 3$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = 4x + 11$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = 12x + 3$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{23 - 11}{5 - 2} = \\frac{12}{3} = 4$. Using the point $(2, 11)$ in point-slope form: $y - 11 = 4(x - 2)$, which gives $y = 4x - 8 + 11 = 4x + 3$. The equation $y = 4x - 3$ has the correct slope but a sign error in the $y$-intercept calculation. The equation $y = 4x + 11$ uses the $y$-coordinate of the first point as the intercept without computing $b$ properly, representing partial reasoning. The equation $y = 12x + 3$ uses the numerator of the slope fraction ($12$) as the slope instead of dividing by the run."}
{"id": "SATM25I8ZUR", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(-2, 11)$ and $(6, -13)$. What is the $x$-intercept of this line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "5/3", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{-13 - 11}{6 - (-2)} = \\frac{-24}{8} = -3$. Using point-slope form with $(-2, 11)$: $y - 11 = -3(x + 2)$, which simplifies to $y = -3x + 5$. Setting $y = 0$ to find the $x$-intercept: $0 = -3x + 5$, so $x = \\frac{5}{3}$."}
{"id": "SATM25MNQL3", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $5x - 2y = 14$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(7, -1)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\frac{9}{5}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{19}{5}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\frac{9}{5}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{5}{2}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $y = \\frac{5}{2}x - 7$, so line $p$ has slope $\\frac{5}{2}$. A perpendicular line has slope $-\\frac{2}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(7, -1)$: $y + 1 = -\\frac{2}{5}(x - 7)$, which gives $y = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{14}{5} - 1 = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{14}{5} - \\frac{5}{5} = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{9}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{9}{5}$. The value $\\frac{19}{5}$ results from adding 1 instead of subtracting when simplifying. The value $-\\frac{9}{5}$ comes from a sign error on the constant term. The value $\\frac{5}{2}$ confuses the slope of line $p$ with the $y$-intercept of line $q$."}
{"id": "SATM25N7SBG", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $5x + 3y = 41$. Line $q$ is parallel to line $p$ and passes through the point $(7, 2)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "41/3", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $3y = -5x + 41$, so $y = -\\frac{5}{3}x + \\frac{41}{3}$. The slope is $-\\frac{5}{3}$. Since line $q$ is parallel, it shares this slope. Using point $(7, 2)$: $2 = -\\frac{5}{3}(7) + b$, giving $2 = -\\frac{35}{3} + b$. Then $b = 2 + \\frac{35}{3} = \\frac{6}{3} + \\frac{35}{3} = \\frac{41}{3}$. The $y$-intercept of line $q$ is $\\frac{41}{3}$."}
{"id": "SATM25OZDEN", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $3x + 8y = 56$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(4, -1)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-\\frac{35}{3}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{29}{3}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\frac{41}{3}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{35}{3}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewrite line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $8y = -3x + 56$, so $y = -\\frac{3}{8}x + 7$ and the slope of $p$ is $-\\frac{3}{8}$. The perpendicular slope is $\\frac{8}{3}$. Using point-slope form with $(4, -1)$: $y - (-1) = \\frac{8}{3}(x - 4)$, giving $y = \\frac{8}{3}x - \\frac{32}{3} - 1 = \\frac{8}{3}x - \\frac{32}{3} - \\frac{3}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}x - \\frac{35}{3}$. The $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{35}{3}$. The value $\\frac{29}{3}$ comes from adding the constant terms instead of subtracting. The value $-\\frac{41}{3}$ results from an arithmetic slip when combining $-\\frac{32}{3}$ and $-\\frac{3}{3}$, getting $-\\frac{41}{3}$ instead of $-\\frac{35}{3}$. The value $\\frac{35}{3}$ drops the negative sign from the correct answer."}
{"id": "SATM25UH1HH", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $y$-intercept of the line $7x + 11y = 77$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$11$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$77$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{77}{7}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The $y$-intercept occurs when $x = 0$. Substituting: $7(0) + 11y = 77$, so $11y = 77$ and $y = 7$. The value 11 results from dividing 77 by 7 (finding the $x$-intercept coefficient) instead of dividing by 11. The value 77 ignores the coefficient of $y$ entirely. The value $\\frac{77}{7}$ finds the $x$-intercept instead of the $y$-intercept."}
{"id": "SATM25XAIDB", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $y$-intercept of the line $2x + y = 8$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-2$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$4$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$8$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-8$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is $8$. To find the $y$-intercept, set $x = 0$ in $2x + y = 8$, which gives $y = 8$. The value $-2$ confuses the slope with the $y$-intercept; rewriting as $y = -2x + 8$ shows the slope is $-2$, not the intercept. The value $4$ results from incorrectly dividing $8$ by the coefficient $2$. The value $-8$ comes from a sign error when identifying the constant term."}
{"id": "SATM261P4YE", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $3x + 5y = 15$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(6, 1)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-9$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-\\frac{19}{3}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{19}{3}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$9$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $-9$. Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $5y = -3x + 15$, so $y = -\\frac{3}{5}x + 3$, and the slope of line $p$ is $-\\frac{3}{5}$. A perpendicular line has slope equal to the negative reciprocal: $\\frac{5}{3}$. Using point-slope form with $(6, 1)$: $y - 1 = \\frac{5}{3}(x - 6)$, so $y = \\frac{5}{3}x - 10 + 1 = \\frac{5}{3}x - 9$. The $y$-intercept is $-9$. $-\\frac{19}{3}$ results from an arithmetic error when computing $\\frac{5}{3} \\cdot 6$. $\\frac{19}{3}$ applies a sign error to the result. $9$ uses the parallel slope instead of the perpendicular slope."}
{"id": "SATM266BQYS", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, -3)$ and $(8, 15)$. What is the equation of the line in slope-intercept form?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = 3x - 9$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 3x + 9$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = -3x + 3$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{11}{3}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{15 - (-3)}{8 - 2} = \\frac{18}{6} = 3$. Using point-slope form with $(2, -3)$: $y + 3 = 3(x - 2) = 3x - 6$, so $y = 3x - 9$. The distractor $y = 3x + 9$ has the correct slope but the wrong intercept sign, from an error simplifying $y + 3 = 3x - 6$. The distractor $y = -3x + 3$ negates the slope by reversing only the numerator in the slope calculation. The distractor $y = \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{11}{3}$ uses the reciprocal of the slope."}
{"id": "SATM266DQ1K", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The equations $5x - 3y = 17$ and $kx + 9y = -51$ represent the same line in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $k$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-15$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-5$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$15$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "For the two equations to represent the same line, one must be a scalar multiple of the other. Comparing $y$-coefficients: $-3 \\cdot c = 9$ gives $c = -3$. Checking constants: $17 \\cdot (-3) = -51$, which matches. Therefore $k = 5 \\cdot (-3) = -15$. The value $15$ results from a sign error, using $c = 3$ instead of $c = -3$. The value $-5$ comes from a concept gap such as simply negating the coefficient of $x$ from the first equation. The value $3$ comes from misreading the ratio as $9 / (-3) = -3$ and then confusing it with $|-3| = 3$."}
{"id": "SATM2672ZIU", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "In the $xy$-plane, the graph of $7x - 4y = k$ passes through the point of intersection of the lines $2x + 3y = 19$ and $4x - y = 3$. What is the value of $k$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-6$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$6$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$23$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$34$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "First, find the intersection of $2x + 3y = 19$ and $4x - y = 3$. From the second equation, $y = 4x - 3$. Substituting into the first: $2x + 3(4x - 3) = 19$, which gives $2x + 12x - 9 = 19$, so $14x = 28$ and $x = 2$. Then $y = 4(2) - 3 = 5$. The intersection point is $(2, 5)$. Substituting into $7x - 4y = k$: $k = 7(2) - 4(5) = 14 - 20 = -6$. The value $6$ results from dropping the negative sign. The value $23$ comes from swapping the intersection coordinates and computing $7(5) - 4(3) = 35 - 12 = 23$. The value $34$ results from computing $7(2) + 4(5) = 14 + 20 = 34$, using addition instead of subtraction."}
{"id": "SATM26E9RN5", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A line is perpendicular to $5x - 3y = 21$ and passes through the point $(-4, 7)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-\\frac{23}{5}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{23}{5}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{47}{5}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{41}{3}$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is $\\frac{23}{5}$. Rewriting the given line in slope-intercept form: $5x - 3y = 21$ becomes $y = \\frac{5}{3}x - 7$, so the slope is $\\frac{5}{3}$. The perpendicular slope is $-\\frac{3}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(-4, 7)$: $y - 7 = -\\frac{3}{5}(x + 4)$, giving $y = -\\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{12}{5} + 7 = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{23}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{23}{5}$. The value $-\\frac{23}{5}$ comes from a sign error when combining $-\\frac{12}{5} + \\frac{35}{5}$, incorrectly getting $-\\frac{23}{5}$. The value $\\frac{47}{5}$ results from dropping the negative sign on the perpendicular slope, using $\\frac{3}{5}$ instead of $-\\frac{3}{5}$, which gives $y = \\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{12}{5} + 7 = \\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{47}{5}$. The value $\\frac{41}{3}$ results from using the original slope $\\frac{5}{3}$ (a parallel line) instead of the perpendicular slope, giving $y = \\frac{5}{3}x + \\frac{41}{3}$."}
