{"id": "SATM27H3D4N", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(-2, 11)$ and $(4, -7)$. Which of the following is the equation of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = -3x + 5$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 3x + 17$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = -3x - 7$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = 3x + 5$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $y = -3x + 5$. First find the slope: $m = \\frac{-7 - 11}{4 - (-2)} = \\frac{-18}{6} = -3$. Using point-slope form with $(-2, 11)$: $y - 11 = -3(x + 2)$, so $y = -3x - 6 + 11 = -3x + 5$. The equation $y = 3x + 17$ results from using a positive slope of $3$ instead of $-3$. The equation $y = -3x - 7$ comes from an arithmetic error when computing the $y$-intercept. The equation $y = 3x + 5$ uses the wrong sign for the slope while getting the correct intercept by coincidence."}
{"id": "SATM27I4EWB", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $5x - 2y = 14$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(7, -3)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "-1/5", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $-2y = -5x + 14$, so $y = \\frac{5}{2}x - 7$. The slope of line $p$ is $\\frac{5}{2}$. A line perpendicular to line $p$ has slope $-\\frac{2}{5}$ (the negative reciprocal). Using point-slope form with the point $(7, -3)$: $y - (-3) = -\\frac{2}{5}(x - 7)$, which gives $y + 3 = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{14}{5}$. Solving for $y$: $y = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{14}{5} - \\frac{15}{5} = -\\frac{2}{5}x - \\frac{1}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{1}{5}$."}
