{"id": "SATM289PHA8", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ passes through the point $(2, 5)$ and is perpendicular to the line $4x + 7y = 21$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $p$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\frac{3}{2}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-\\frac{3}{2}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{7}{2}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{13}{4}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting $4x + 7y = 21$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = -\\frac{4}{7}x + 3$, so the slope is $-\\frac{4}{7}$. The perpendicular slope is $\\frac{7}{4}$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 5)$: $y - 5 = \\frac{7}{4}(x - 2)$, which simplifies to $y = \\frac{7}{4}x - \\frac{14}{4} + \\frac{20}{4} = \\frac{7}{4}x + \\frac{3}{2}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{3}{2}$. The value $-\\frac{3}{2}$ results from a sign error when combining $-\\frac{14}{4}$ and $\\frac{20}{4}$. The value $\\frac{7}{2}$ comes from using the original slope $-\\frac{4}{7}$ instead of the perpendicular slope. The value $\\frac{13}{4}$ results from an incorrect point-slope setup, such as using $(2, 5)$ with the wrong slope formula."}
