{"id": "SATM28QOVK8", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line in the $xy$-plane passes through the points $(3, 13)$ and $(7, -3)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$25$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-25$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-31$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The slope of the line is $\\frac{-3 - 13}{7 - 3} = \\frac{-16}{4} = -4$. Using point-slope form with the point $(3, 13)$: $y - 13 = -4(x - 3)$, which simplifies to $y = -4x + 12 + 13 = -4x + 25$. The $y$-intercept is $25$. The value $1$ results from an arithmetic slip such as computing $13 - 12$ instead of $13 + 12$. The value $-25$ results from a sign error applied to the correct magnitude. The value $-31$ results from using a positive slope of $4$ with the point $(7, -3)$: $y + 3 = 4(x - 7)$, giving $y = 4x - 31$."}
{"id": "SATM28T28XD", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "What is the $y$-intercept of the line $3x + 7y = 42$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(0, -6)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(0, 6)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(0, 14)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(14, 0)$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The $y$-intercept occurs where $x = 0$. Substituting $x = 0$ into $3(0) + 7y = 42$ gives $7y = 42$, so $y = 6$. The $y$-intercept is $(0, 6)$. The point $(0, -6)$ reflects a sign error in the final step. The point $(0, 14)$ comes from dividing $42$ by $3$ instead of by $7$, confusing the coefficients. The point $(14, 0)$ is the $x$-intercept, not the $y$-intercept, resulting from setting $y = 0$ instead of $x = 0$."}
